T1  - Comprehending possible manifestations and design aspects of embodied intelligence in next-generation cyber-physical systems
AU  - Horváth, Imre
JO  - Design and Artificial Intelligence
SP  - 100097
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/08/06/
SN  - 3050-7413
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.daai.2026.100097
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S3050741326000364
KW  - Systems design
KW  - Cyber-physical systems
KW  - Next generation systems
KW  - Embodied intelligence
KW  - Theoretical fundamentals
KW  - Implementation possibilities
KW  - Application opportunities
AB  - The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping the conceptual, architectural, and methodological foundations of cyber-physical systems (CPSs) featuring sensorimotor control. While generative and agentic AI have extended digital reasoning capabilities, embodied artificial intelligence (EAI) introduces a qualitatively distinct paradigm in which cognition is partially instantiated in morphology, materials, dynamics, and environmental interaction. This article develops a comprehensive theoretical and design-oriented framework for understanding and operationalizing embodiment in next-generation cyber-physical systems (NG-CPSs). The paper proceeds in six stages. First, it reconstructs the ontological evolution of CPSs from control-centric architectures to ecosystemic cognitive-material systems. Second, it articulates a systematic distinction between digital artificial intelligence (DAI) and EAI, clarifying their epistemological and functional differences. Third, it analyzes morphological computation and hybrid control architectures as foundational mechanisms of embodiment. Fourth, it formulates a taxonomy of embodiment-relevant system requirements and introduces the degree of embodiment (DoE) as a formal design metric. Fifth, it integrates embodied and agentic AI into a unified architectural framework. Sixth, it examines methodological, economic, and ethical implications of EAI encapsulated in the NG-CPS designs. The conceptual arguments are illustrated through the case of a full-body stroke rehabilitation (FBSR) system, demonstrating how embodied cognition enables real-time biomechanical co-regulation, adaptive proprioceptive scaffolding, and safe human–machine interaction beyond purely digital control. This position paper makes three original contributions. First, it formulates a unified theoretical framework that interprets embodiment as a distributed cognitive phenomenon spanning morphology, materials, dynamics, environment, and digital intelligence within NG-CPSs. Second, it introduces the DoE for evaluating the extent to which cognition is physically instantiated in NG-CPSs. Third, it demonstrates how EAI enables functionalities that cannot be implemented effectively through purely DAI deployment. The article concludes that strong embodiment is not universally necessary. Rather, its implementation should be justified by structural, functional, safety, environmental, and economic constraints. The findings support a paradigm shift from controlling physics with computation toward “computing” through physics in the design of NG-CPSs.
ER  - 

