KW  - Agentic AI
KW  - Software architecture
KW  - Architectural tactics
KW  - Neuro-symbolic methods
KW  - Trustworthy AI
KW  - Industrial case study
AB  - Context:
Agentic AI components, such as autonomous reviewers and refactoring tools, are increasingly embedded in development toolchains, serving as key architectural elements governing software quality. In data-centric systems like Manufacturing Execution Systems (MES), these components must address the complexity of Cross-Layer behaviors spanning volatile application code and persistent database artifacts.
Objective:
We frame code review as an architectural capability of Agentic AI and propose tactics to bridge the “Semantic Gap” between application logic and database behavior, mitigating risks associated with AI hallucinations.
Method:
We introduce a Neuro-Symbolic framework that constructs a deterministic Cross-Layer Dependency Graph (CLDG), linking application methods to database side effects. This symbolic context grounds Generative AI agents, enabling them to provide verifiable explanations and accurate repair suggestions.
Results:
In a 3-month industrial pilot (342 pull requests), the system identified 20 high-severity vulnerabilities (12 SQL injections, 8 potential deadlocks) missed by the baseline pipeline, achieving 87.0% precision. The framework significantly reduced average review iterations from 2.4 to 1.1. Proactive refactoring suggestions achieved a 71% overall acceptance rate, with developer trust and adoption exhibiting statistically significant improvements over time (p<0.001 and p<0.01, Wilcoxon signed-rank test).
Conclusion:
Treating Agentic AI as a structural component and coupling it with deterministic Cross-Layer context significantly enhances review coverage, operational efficiency, and trustworthiness in data-centric enterprise environments.
ER  - 

